# The ledger of `i18n/dates`: dates and times written and read as a
# language writes them, Gregorian, from CLDR 49's data for each of its 657
# locales. One block per capability. The census is the one `i18n.ledger`
# names: Go's `golang.org/x/text` (which writes no dates), Python 3.15.2's
# `locale`, Java SE 25's `java.text.DateFormat`, `SimpleDateFormat` and
# `DateFormatSymbols` and `java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter`, .NET's
# `DateTimeFormatInfo` and `Calendar`, ICU 79.1 the yardstick. The tests hold
# 600 cases drawn from 10,914 that ICU answered over every locale both carry
# (`icu_cases`), the Gregorian calendar asked; 29 differ: 27 skeletons of
# locales whose data ICU inherits from the root otherwise, Hawaiian's short
# date (its month in lower-case Roman numerals, a numbering override not
# read), and UTC named in Latin by ICU in sr-Cyrl-ME.

capability date/style  a date, a time or both written in a language's full, long, medium or short form
  heroes  dates.format_date, dates.format_time, dates.format_date_time, dates.DateStyle
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  locale.nl_langinfo(D_FMT, T_FMT, D_T_FMT, T_FMT_AMPM, ERA_D_FMT, ERA_D_T_FMT, ERA_T_FMT)
  java    DateFormat.getDateInstance, DateFormat.getTimeInstance, DateFormat.getDateTimeInstance, DateTimeFormatter.ofLocalizedDate, ofLocalizedTime, ofLocalizedDateTime, FormatStyle
  dotnet  DateTime.ToString("D", "d", "T", "t", "F", "f", "G", "g"), DateTimeFormatInfo.LongDatePattern, ShortDatePattern, LongTimePattern, ShortTimePattern, FullDateTimePattern
  icu     DateFormat.createDateTimeInstance, udat_open
  note    a date and a time are joined by CLDR's at-time pattern for every style where the language has one, as ICU 79 joins them

capability date/pattern  a moment written by a CLDR pattern
  heroes  dates.format_pattern
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  java    SimpleDateFormat(String, Locale), SimpleDateFormat.format, DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(String, Locale)
  dotnet  DateTime.ToString(String, IFormatProvider)
  icu     SimpleDateFormat, udat_open(UDAT_PATTERN)
  note    every field of UTS #35 but U (cyclic years) and the zone's names in words, which are written in the language's GMT form

capability date/skeleton  a moment written with the fields asked, in the language's order and forms
  heroes  dates.format_skeleton, dates.skeleton_pattern
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  java    DateTimeFormatter.ofLocalizedPattern
  dotnet  -
  icu     DateTimePatternGenerator.getBestPattern, udatpg_getBestPattern
  note    ICU's weighing: the locale's own available formats, then its standard patterns, then those it inherits from the root; a weekday asked with E is abbreviated at least; fields joined with the at-time glue

capability date/patterns  a language's own patterns
  heroes  dates.date_pattern, dates.time_pattern, dates.date_time_pattern
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  locale.nl_langinfo(D_FMT, T_FMT, D_T_FMT)
  java    SimpleDateFormat.toPattern, DateTimeFormatterBuilder.getLocalizedDateTimePattern
  dotnet  DateTimeFormatInfo.GetAllDateTimePatterns, LongDatePattern, ShortTimePattern
  icu     udat_toPattern

capability date/parse  a date or time written in a language read back
  heroes  dates.parse_pattern
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  java    DateFormat.parse, SimpleDateFormat.parse, DateTimeFormatter.parse
  dotnet  DateTime.Parse(String, IFormatProvider), DateTime.ParseExact
  icu     udat_parse
  note    names of every width and both contexts ignoring case, ISO and GMT offsets; a two-digit year within 1921 to 2020

capability date/names  month, weekday, quarter, day period and era names
  heroes  dates.month_names, dates.weekday_names, dates.quarter_names, dates.day_period_names, dates.era_names, dates.Width, dates.NameContext
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  locale.nl_langinfo(DAY_1..7, ABDAY_1..7, MON_1..12, ABMON_1..12, AM_STR, PM_STR, ERA)
  java    DateFormatSymbols.getMonths, getShortMonths, getWeekdays, getShortWeekdays, getAmPmStrings, getEras, DateFormatSymbols.getInstance
  dotnet  DateTimeFormatInfo.MonthNames, AbbreviatedMonthNames, MonthGenitiveNames, DayNames, AbbreviatedDayNames, ShortestDayNames, AMDesignator, PMDesignator, GetEraName
  icu     DateFormatSymbols
  note    weekdays Monday first, as `time.Weekday` counts them

capability date/week  the first day of the week and the minimal days of the first week
  heroes  dates.first_weekday, dates.minimal_days
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  java    WeekFields.of(Locale), Calendar.getFirstDayOfWeek, Calendar.getMinimalDaysInFirstWeek
  dotnet  DateTimeFormatInfo.FirstDayOfWeek, CalendarWeekRule
  icu     Calendar.getFirstDayOfWeek, ucal_getAttribute

capability date/hour-cycle  whether a region writes a 12-hour clock
  heroes  dates.uses_12_hour_clock
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  locale.nl_langinfo(T_FMT_AMPM)
  java    -
  dotnet  -
  icu     DateTimePatternGenerator.getDefaultHourCycle

capability date/relative  a time relative to now, "in 3 days", "yesterday"
  heroes  dates.format_relative, dates.format_relative_named, dates.RelativeUnit, dates.RelativeStyle
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  java    -
  dotnet  -
  icu     RelativeDateTimeFormatter, ureldatefmt_formatNumeric, ureldatefmt_format
  note    every amount the language groups from four digits, as ICU's relative formatter does

capability date/relative-chosen  a time relative to now in a unit chosen for the span, "5 minutes ago", "in 2 hours", "yesterday"
  heroes  dates.format_relative_duration, dates.format_relative_duration_named, dates.format_relative_instant, dates.format_relative_instant_named
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  java    -
  dotnet  -
  icu     -
  note    ICU's `RelativeDateTimeFormatter` and ECMA-402's `Intl.RelativeTimeFormat` take the unit from the caller; Swift's `RelativeDateTimeFormatter.localizedString(for:relativeTo:)` chooses it, and is the yardstick: the largest unit of which the span holds one whole, whole ones counted toward zero, observed 2026-11-21 with Swift 6.4.2 at 46 spans from 0 to 9e9 seconds, each both ways, numeric and named: 37 alike in all four forms, and every span under four weeks among them; the 9 that differ are at 28 days and beyond, where Swift counts on the calendar from the day asked (2026-03-08), writing "1 month ago" for 28 days back and "in 1 year" for 365 days, and this on the Gregorian averages, a month 30.436875 days and a year 365.2425, so a span has one answer whatever day it ends on and a `time.Duration`, which has no calendar, is counted as two instants are; the common JavaScript rule over `Intl.RelativeTimeFormat` (divide by 60, 60, 24, 7, 4.34524 and 12, then round) rounds where this counts down, and writes "in 60 seconds" for 59.6 seconds, "in 60 minutes" for 3,599 and "in 12 months" for 365 days; Python's `humanize`, not installed here, was not asked; Java and .NET unrun, neither installed here

capability date/zone-names  a time zone named in words, "Central European Time"
  heroes  pending: CLDR's metazone names are about 50 KB a locale; a zone is written by its offset in the language's GMT form, and UTC by its own name
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  java    TimeZone.getDisplayName, DateFormatSymbols.getZoneStrings
  dotnet  TimeZoneInfo.DisplayName, StandardName, DaylightName
  icu     TimeZoneNames, TimeZoneFormat

capability date/calendars  calendars other than the Gregorian: Buddhist, Hebrew, Islamic, Japanese, Persian
  heroes  pending: only the Gregorian is written; a locale whose default calendar is another (Persian in Iran) is written Gregorian
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  java    Calendar.getInstance(Locale), HijrahChronology, JapaneseChronology, ThaiBuddhistChronology
  dotnet  HijriCalendar, HebrewCalendar, JapaneseCalendar, PersianCalendar, ThaiBuddhistCalendar, UmAlQuraCalendar, GregorianCalendar
  icu     Calendar, ucal_open

capability date/interval  a range of dates, "March 5–7, 2024"
  heroes  pending: CLDR's interval formats are not carried
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  java    -
  dotnet  -
  icu     DateIntervalFormat

capability date/duration  a duration written in words, "3 hours, 5 minutes"
  heroes  dates.format_duration, dates.duration_parts, dates.DurationPart, dates.DurationUnit, dates.DurationStyle
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  java    -
  dotnet  -
  icu     MeasureFormat.formatMeasures, ListFormatter
  note    CLDR 49's unit patterns of ten units of time and its unit list patterns, for each of the 657 locales, carried by `unit_tables`, which `generators/i18n_units/` writes and checks; ECMA-402's `Intl.DurationFormat` in Node 26.13.0 (ICU 79.1) and ICU 79.1's `MeasureFormat` are the yardsticks: over the 637 locales of the 657 that ICU carries as themselves, 17 durations each in three widths, 32,442 of 32,487 answers alike on 2026-11-21, the 45 others all `sr-Cyrl-ME`, which ICU writes in Latin letters and CLDR 49's data in Cyrillic, followed here; where the two yardsticks part: a part of 0 is written, as ICU writes it where ECMA-402 leaves it out and answers "" for a duration of zeros; a negative duration's sign stands once, on its first part, as ECMA-402 writes it where ICU signs every part, and mixed signs, which ECMA-402 refuses, sign each part as ICU does; counts are grouped from CLDR's minimum grouping digits, "1234 horas", as ECMA-402 groups them where ICU's `MeasureFormat` writes "1.234 horas"; Go's `Duration.String`, Rust's `Duration` `Debug` and Python's `timedelta` write no words; Java and .NET unrun, neither installed here

capability date/relative-compound  a span of several units relative to now, "7 minutes and 30 seconds ago"
  heroes  refused: CLDR 49 holds no pattern for it in any locale, its relative patterns each taking one unit's number (every key of every locale's date, unit and list files read, 2026-11-21); ICU 79.1's `RelativeDateTimeFormatter` (every format method of its header takes one quantity and one unit, and `combineDateAndTime` joins a named day and a time), ECMA-402's `Intl.RelativeTimeFormat` (a RangeError for any other unit) and Swift's `RelativeDateTimeFormatter` (given 7 minutes and 30 seconds back, "7 minutes ago") write none; and a sentence assembled from a duration and a word of another language is wrong in many, as Swift's `DateComponentsFormatter` showed here, writing "7 minuti e 30 secondi remaining". A program writes `dates.format_duration`'s words inside a sentence of its own, in a language it controls
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  java    -
  dotnet  -
  icu     -

dropped plumbing  mutable formatters, fields and calendars as objects
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  locale.LC_TIME
  java    DateFormat.Field, DateFormat.setTimeZone, DateFormat.setLenient, DateFormat.setCalendar, SimpleDateFormat.set2DigitYearStart, FieldPosition, ParsePosition, DateFormatSymbols.setMonths and its setters, DateTimeFormatter.withZone, DateTimeFormatter.withLocale
  dotnet  DateTimeFormatInfo.Clone, ReadOnly, InvariantInfo, Calendar, DateSeparator, TimeSeparator, GetFormat
  icu     -
